"""Preserve local notation and source limitations without rewriting theorems."""
import json
from pathlib import Path
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];PID=ROOT.name
aux=[]
def add(aid,text,pages,loc,meaning):aux.append(dict(auxiliary_id=aid,statement_original=text.strip(),evidence=[dict(page=p,location=loc) for p in pages],resolution=meaning))
add('A1',r'''
Here, $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{test}}$ is a measure of the adversary’s power and $\varepsilon$ is the “perceived” adversary’s power used by the algorithm. Our theory allows for $\varepsilon$ to be different from $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{test}}$; cf Theorem 4.2. In our numerical experiments in Section 2 we consider $\varepsilon=\varepsilon_{\mathrm{test}}$ to focus on other relevant quantities, namely $\psi_1$, $\psi_2$ on adversarial risk.
''',[11],'Section 4 — training and test radii','The risk limit allows distinct training and test radii. The equality imposed in some numerical plots is not a theorem assumption.')
add('A2',r'''Here, $\boldsymbol 1=(1,1,\ldots,1)\in\mathbb R^N$ and $\zeta>0$ is an arbitrary small but fixed constant.''',[15],'After equation (6.1) — all-ones vector','The all-ones vector used in Theorem 6.9 is defined here. Its reuse does not import the auxiliary loss, Omega matrix, or zeta limit into the theorem.')
add('A3',r'''
We use $O_d(\cdot)$, $o_d(\cdot)$ to denote the standard big-O and little-o notation, where we stress the asymptotic variable $d$. Likewise, we use $O_{d,\mathbb P}$ and $o_{d,\mathbb P}$ to indicate asymptotic behavior in probability. Specifically, $f(d)=O_{d,\mathbb P}(g(d))$ if for any $\varepsilon>0$, there exists $C_\varepsilon>0$ and large enough $d_\varepsilon$ such that $P(|f(d)/g(d)|>C_\varepsilon)\leq\varepsilon$, for all $d\geq d_\varepsilon$. Similarly, $f(d)=o_{d,\mathbb P}(g(d))$ if $f(d)/g(d)$ converges to zero in probability. We write $f(d)\approx g(d)$ as $d\to\infty$, when $f(d)-g(d)\to0$, in probability.
''',[15,16],'Section 6 — asymptotic notation','The limit variable is d with dimensions growing as specified in the separate proportional-growth passage. A difference tending to zero is distinct from ratio convergence.')
add('A4',r'''
For a matrix $\boldsymbol A$, we denote by $\|\boldsymbol A\|$ its operator norm, $\|\boldsymbol A\|_F=(\sum_{ij}A_{ij}^2)^{1/2}$ the Frobenius norm of $\boldsymbol A$. For an integer $n$, we use the shorthand $[n]=\{1,\ldots,n\}$.

Finally, the indicator function is denoted by $\mathbb I(\cdot)$ – i.e., $\mathbb I(A)=1$ only if the event $A$ holds true, and otherwise $\mathbb I(A)=0$.
''',[16],'Section 6 — norms and indicators','Resolves the spectral norm in E_W and componentwise sign indicators in the J matrix. The Hadamard product and entrywise inverse cosine in (6.10) have their matrix-entry interpretation; no appendix notation is needed.')
add('A5',r'''for a large enough constant $C$ (note that $N/d:=\psi_{1,d}$ – see Assumption 1).''',[15],'After equation (6.2) — finite aspect ratio','The finite ratio psi_(1,d) in E_W is N/d. Using that notation does not import the signal normalization part of Assumption 1.')
add('A6',r'''In the following, our statements are proven conditioned on the event $\mathcal E_{\boldsymbol W}$ which holds with high probability.''',[15],'Section 6 — conditional scope','Theorem 6.9 expressly assumes E_W and is read with this section convention. Theorem 4.2 explicitly states probability over both W and the training data and is not turned into a conditional theorem by its proof strategy.')
add('A7',r'''Let $\eta_i(\boldsymbol\theta):=\|\boldsymbol W^\top\operatorname{diag}(\mathbb I(\boldsymbol W\boldsymbol x_i>0))\boldsymbol\theta\|_{\ell_2}$''',[17],'Proposition 6.4 — definition fragment','Resolves the auxiliary quantity in the displayed interpretation E[eta_i(theta)^2]=||J theta||^2. Only this definition fragment is needed; the proposition\'s uniform concentration claim and its coefficient constraint set are not imported.')
add('A8',r'''where the expectation is with respect to $\boldsymbol x_i$ and $\boldsymbol W$ is fixed.''',[17],'After equation (6.9) — conditional matrix expectation','J averages over a fresh Gaussian covariate at fixed W. Its defining matrix square root is the positive-semidefinite square root, as stated in the next paragraph.')
add('A9',r'''where $\boldsymbol f_i$ are generated i.i.d. according to (6.15).''',[19],'After equation (6.16) — surrogate sample','The noisy linear features used in Theorem 6.9 are the sample-indexed copies of (6.15). W is shared across the sample, so this construction is interpreted with W held fixed, consistently with the section convention.')
